Q: How to resolve WMQ java.lang.ClassNotFoundException: com.ibm.mq.jms.MQConnectionFactory error? A: <p>You added all related JARs to your local environment (your IDE build path), but said JARs are not packaged with your application, thus not available on CloudHub upon deployment and causing your error. As mentioned on the <a href="https://docs.mulesoft.com/runtime-manager/developing-applications-for-cloudhub#packaging-third-party-libraries" rel="nofollow noreferrer">CloudHub documentation</a>, you need to make sure your third party JARs are placed into the <code>/lib</code> folder of your app package. </p>

<p>I recommend you add all required JARs as Maven <code>&lt;dependency&gt;</code>, thus your JARs will be included in your Mule application archive <code>/lib</code> folder by Maven (you can then remove them from your IDE's classpath, they will be automatically added under M2_REPO/...). For example for <code>mqjms</code>:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;com.ibm&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;com.ibm.mqjms&lt;/artifactId&gt;
    &lt;version&gt;5.3.07&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>The tricky part is that most of your JARs (IBM libs) are proprietary and not available on Public Maven repositories. You may need to:</p>

<ul>
<li>Make sure they are available on your entreprise repo (if you have any) to allow you and your team members to use them. See <a href="https://maven.apache.org/guides/mini/guide-3rd-party-jars-remote.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Guide to deploying 3rd party JARs to remote repository</a>. <em>(Note: I don't know licensing regarding these JARs, it may not allow such deployment)</em></li>
<li>If you do not have a private entreprise repo available, simply install these JARs on your local repository. See <a href="https://maven.apache.org/guides/mini/guide-3rd-party-jars-local.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Guide to installing 3rd party JARs </a></li>
</ul>

<p>You also have the possibility to add them manually or using another way, the important thing being that upon deployment on CloudHub these JARs are in the app <code>/lib</code> folder.</p>
